  "body": "### What is the issue with the HTML Standard?\n\nhttps://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/rendering.html#flow-content-3\r\n\r\n> The [`div`](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/grouping-content.html#the-div-element) element, when it has an [align](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/obsolete.html#attr-div-align) attribute whose value is an [ASCII case-insensitive](https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#ascii-case-insensitive) match for the string \"`justify`\", is expected to full-justify text within itself, as if it had its ['text-align'](https://drafts.csswg.org/css-text/#text-align-property) property set to 'justify' in a [presentational hint](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/rendering.html#presentational-hints), **and to [align descendants](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/rendering.html#align-descendants) to the left**.\r\n\r\nHowever, all browsers I tested (Gecko, Blink, WebKit, Servo 2013) align to the start side, not to the left side. Testcase:\r\n\r\n```html\r\n<!DOCTYPE html>\r\n<div align=\"justify\" style=\"border: solid; direction: rtl\">\r\n  <div style=\"width: 150px; background: cyan\">\r\n    Lorem ipsum dolor sit amet, consectetur adipiscing elit, sed do eiusmod tempor incididunt ut labore et dolore magna aliqua.\r\n  </div>\r\n</div>\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe cyan box appears on the right, not on the left. I think the spec should be changed to reflect reality?",
